We are inviting a small set of external reviewers to look at the Sex & Gender package in the Health-RI Ontology (HRIO), developed within the Health-RI Semantic Interoperability Initiative.

A recurring problem in interoperability work is “false agreement”: two systems may record the same value (e.g., “male”) but intend different meanings (for example, biological sex vs legal sex). We are trying to make those meanings explicit so standards and local models can align through reusable mappings, instead of depending on labels (or keeping many pairwise mappings in sync).

If you have time to take a look, I’d especially value your perspective on whether the model:

* captures the domain correctly and with appropriate boundaries,
* uses terminology that is clear and not misleading, and
* makes key assumptions sufficiently explicit for others to reuse.

You can start here: https://health-ri.github.io/semantic-interoperability/external-review/sex-and-gender
(The page links the package documentation and the OWL specification, and explains how we’re collecting feedback.)

We are collecting comments over the next few weeks, but feedback is welcome at any time. If you prefer not to be acknowledged, just say so and we will handle it anonymously.

